WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.150 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  u.s president joe biden has urged the  country to use show unity while honoring  the victims of the 9 11 attacks it's  been 20 years since hijacked passenger  planes were flown into the world trade  center in new york and the pentagon just  outside washington dc three thousand  people were killed rob reynolds looks  back at the aftermath of those events  a whisper in the president's ear set in  motion actions that changed the u.s and  the world  three days after the attacks president  george bush stood atop the rubble of  lower manhattan and promised retribution  i can hear you the rest of the world  hears you  and the people  [Applause]  and the people who knock these buildings  down  will hear all of us soon  blame immediately fell on osama bin  laden's al qaeda organization based in  afghanistan which had already carried  out several deadly attacks on u.s  targets abroad less than a month after  the attacks the u.s began military  action in afghanistan bin laden escaped  but was tracked down in pakistan nearly  a decade later the united states has  conducted an operation that killed osama  bin laden the leader of al qaeda  but even with bin laden dead the war in  afghanistan dragged on for another 10  years ending in a taliban victory the  september 11 attacks were a profound  shock with images playing out on tv  in the aftermath lawmakers rushed to  overhaul national security laws with  little or no debate  the events of 9 11 really frightened  people enough that i don't think many  people ever read the patriot act and  realized  really how profoundly it changed what  government is able to do  in terms of tracking and conducting  surveillance on american citizens  for many americans the closest contact  with the new enhanced security state  came at the airport the magnetometer the  metal detector the x-ray machines  you had to start  having your id scrutinized  and then taking off your shoes the  limitations on liquids and then finally  in 2010 the full body scanner things  that few americans would have  contemplated before now were widely  accepted the government initially was  intercepting virtually all of the  metadata on all of our emails and  telephone conversations without any  warrants this was just dragnet  surveillance  the invasion of iraq in 2003 overthrew  saddam hussein who was later captured  and executed but it produced no weapons  of mass destruction  two hundred thousand iraqis and more  than four thousand u.s troops were  killed the naval base at guantanamo bay  cuba became a prison where so-called  enemy combatants were held indefinitely  some remain there to this day  a color-coded system of threats was  introduced keeping citizens on edge that  was the goal frighten the american  people you know inflate the risk factor  a million fold and then people say oh  well we have to accept this because  otherwise there'll be bodies on the  sidewalks and on the streets and  everywhere else the abuse of detainees  by u.s soldiers at abu ghraib prison in  iraq appalled many in the u.s and around  the globe  but at the same time in the u.s  veneration of the military grew we have  sort of fetishized the military over the  last 20 years we have no idea of the  long-term impact  of having people who've grown up  entirely during a war period or people  whose entire adult life has been serving  in a war zone  four presidents and two wars later the  u.s is a far different country from the  one that existed 20 years ago  rob reynolds al jazeera  memorial services will be held across  the u.s to mark the 9 11 attacks emily  anguin looks back at how that day  unfolded 20 years ago  it began as a clear sunny tuesday  morning along america's east coast just  before 8 o'clock on september 11 2001  american airlines flight 11 takes off  from boston followed by united airlines  flight 175.  a short while later american airlines  flight 77 departs from washington's  dulles airport and united airlines  flight 93 from newark new jersey  at 8 46 a.m flight 11 crashes into the  north tower of the world trade center in  new york people assume it's a terrible  accident more than a thousand first  responders head to the scene  17 minutes later flight 175  crashes into the south tower president  bush is informed that america is under  attack american airlines flight 77 then  crashes into the pentagon authorities  ground all flights across the united  states but one more plane was still in  the air united 93 was headed for  washington dc possibly the white house  capitol building or other targets after  burning for 56 minutes the south tower  of the world trade center collapses  morning networks are showing these  pictures live to millions of americans  the passengers and crew on united 93  overpower the hijackers and the plane  crashes into a field in shanksville  pennsylvania  at 10 28 am the north tower of the world  trade center also collapses nearly 3 000  people were killed including citizens  from more than 90 countries

